WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:10.360
JOE KREITZ: One of the greatest
bottlenecks in the area of gene

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.130
therapy is delivery.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.990
If you can't deliver
a therapeutic payload

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.700
into a diseased
cell, you sort of

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.410
have no hope of curing that
cell in any meaningful way.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.600
And so in attempting
to solve this problem,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.240
we are highly
inspired by viruses,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.220
which, through the
process of evolution,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.870
have developed elegant
methods by which

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.130
to infiltrate host cells.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.260
And one method used by a
certain class of viruses

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.290
that infect bacteria are called
bacteriophages, and here's

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.970
a little bacteriophage virus.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:37.600
And so these phages utilize this
really elegant macromolecular

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.490
syringe device.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.990
And here's this kind of syringe
structure beneath the capsid

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:41.860
of the virus.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.440
And they use these syringes
to sort of directly

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.540
puncture their host membranes.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.780
And this device is known as a
contractil injection system,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.250
this kind of syringe structure.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.230
In a manner similar to
the action of a virus

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.540
injecting its genome
into a host cell,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.270
we are developing
contractile injection systems

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.880
capable of injecting
therapeutic proteins directly

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.780
into human cells.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.490
And through this
project, we first

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.760
successfully purified a novel
contractile injection system

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.310
from a laboratory
organism in E. coli.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.990
We next show that
the system is capable

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:15.590
injecting large protein cargos
into cultured eukaryotic cells.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.350
Finally, we're
currently reprogramming

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.290
the system for injection
against cultured human cells

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.000
with the ultimate
intention of developing

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.070
safe and specific
therapeutic delivery

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:27.040
devices with these
contracted injection systems

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.360
to enable the next
generation of gene therapy.

